    "description": "A small woodland hawk with short wings and a long tail. Confirmed in Georgia as a year-round resident and passage migrant on the 2024 national bird checklist.",
    "overview": "The Eurasian sparrowhawk (Accipiter nisus) is a small woodland hawk confirmed in Georgia as a year-round resident and a passage migrant. The 2024 annotated checklist of the birds of Georgia codes it YR-R; M: a breeder present throughout the year, and also a bird of passage in spring and autumn. Seasonality is classed as high-quality (code A). Occurrence quality is high (H), with photographic evidence. The usual Georgian name is მიმინო; ქორი is a different species (northern goshawk). Adults are about 29–41 cm long at species level; females are clearly larger than males. It is not a threat to people. IUCN lists the species as Least Concern. The 2024 checklist assigns the same category nationally.",
    "habitat": "This page does not colour a municipality-by-municipality map. The 2024 checklist confirms country-level resident and migrant status but does not publish a regional gazetteer. Do not assume it occurs evenly across Georgia, including treeless high plateaus. Abuladze (2011, 2013) treats it in Georgia as a common forest breeder, a widespread passage migrant, and a winter visitor, with wintering birds often in towns and villages. Named locality: the Agency of Protected Areas lists the species among raptors of Batsara-Babaneuri Protected Areas (Akhmeta Municipality). In autumn the eastern Black Sea bottleneck near Batumi (Sakhalvasho, Shuamta) is a main watchpoint; birds pass low and solitarily there, so complete counts are difficult. Species-level habitat is woodland — deciduous, coniferous, or mixed — with forest edge, parks, orchards, and urban greenery from which it hunts by surprise. A separate national elevation band for Georgia is not published here.",
    "diet": "There is no separate Georgian diet study on this page. Across the range it takes almost exclusively birds: males smaller prey (tits, finches, sparrows), females larger (thrushes and thrush-sized birds; occasionally a pigeon). Small mammals appear rarely in species-level literature; that is not a measured Georgian menu. It hunts by surprise, a short dash from cover, often at garden feeders or woodland edge. It is not food for people.",
    "behavior": "It is active by day. Flight is a flap-flap-glide; the silhouette is T-shaped, with a long, almost square-ended tail and blunt wing-tips. It is highly manoeuvrable in trees. The call is a sharp, rapid kek-kek-kek or similar series, more often around the nest; prey alarm calls are often heard first. Do not handle it. The nest is a stick platform in a tree. Species-level clutches are usually four to five eggs, with incubation of about a month; those figures are from the wider range, not a Georgian nest sample on this page. The 2024 checklist treats the species as a breeding resident in Georgia. Northern populations are migratory; southern birds are more sedentary. Georgia has both: local breeders and a passage flow, especially in autumn at the Batumi bottleneck.",
    "conservation": "BirdLife International assesses Accipiter nisus as Least Concern on the IUCN Red List, with a stable trend. The 2024 Georgian bird checklist assigns global and national Red List category LC. A current national census is not given on this page. Abuladze’s 2011 summary spoke of several hundred breeding pairs; that is a period estimate, not a new national count. The 2014 Georgian Red List (Decree No. 190) includes Levant sparrowhawk (Accipiter brevipes, VU), not this species. Accipiter nisus still appears on state wildlife-accounting lists. Documented local pressures are illegal shooting and traditional trapping for falconry in Ajara at the Batumi bottleneck. Do not catch wild birds without a permit. Globally, organochlorine pesticides once thinned eggshells; after bans, many populations recovered. That is not a Georgian monitoring series.",
    "interaction": "It is not a threat to people. It may take small birds in gardens. Wild birds must not be captured; the talons are strong.",
