    "facts": [
      "Described by Linnaeus in 1758 in Systema Naturae. The 2024 Georgian checklist follows the OSME Region List for names: order Accipitriformes, family Accipitridae, genus Accipiter. IOC World Bird List keeps Accipiter nisus in Accipiter; northern goshawk is now Astur gentilis and Levant sparrowhawk Tachyspiza brevipes. Field identification is to species; the checklist still uses the older genus names.",
      "Handbooks assign Caucasus birds to the nominate subspecies, A. n. nisus (Europe and Asia Minor east to western Siberia and Iran). Eastern A. n. nisosimilis is a more Siberian form and is not a field split in Georgia. Macaronesian A. n. granti does not occur here.",
      "The usual Georgian name is მიმინო; this page title, ქორისებრი მიმინო, marks the hawk family (Accipitridae). English names in use are Eurasian Sparrowhawk (2024 checklist), northern sparrowhawk, and sparrowhawk. Russian is ястреб-перепелятник. ქორი (goshawk) and ქორცქვიტა (Levant sparrowhawk) are other species.",
      "2024 checklist codes: YR-R; M, data quality A, global and national Red List LC, occurrence quality H, evidence PH (photograph). That is country-level confirmation plus the named literature and localities above, not a complete regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small hawk with short broad wings, a long tail, and yellow eyes. The male is blue-grey above with rufous barring below; the female is larger and browner. In Georgia the usual confusion is Levant sparrowhawk on migration and northern goshawk by size.",
      "traits": [
        "Silhouette: short, rounded wings and a long, almost square-ended tail. Flight is flap-flap-glide, not the pointed wings of a falcon. The adult eye is yellow to orange; the legs are long and yellow. Length about 29–41 cm at species level. Males about 29–34 cm and 105–196 g, females 35–41 cm and 185–350 g — among the largest size gaps between the sexes in raptors. A separate measured series from Georgia is not given on this page.",
        "The male is slate blue-grey above, with rufous-barred cheeks and breast; the underparts can look plain orange at a distance. The female is dark brown or grey-brown above, brown-barred below, often with a clearer supercilium. Juveniles resemble the female but the barring is coarser or blotchy, and the eye is paler yellow. Juveniles usually lack a dark mid-throat stripe — a Levant sparrowhawk mark.",
        "Levant sparrowhawk (Accipiter brevipes; IOC now Tachyspiza brevipes) is a breeding migrant in Georgia on the 2024 checklist (BB; M) and is VU on the national Red List. It has more pointed wings, dark wing-tips, a less barred underwing, and a dark adult eye, not yellow. It often migrates in flocks; Eurasian sparrowhawk usually passes singly. Northern goshawk (Accipiter gentilis; IOC Astur gentilis) is much bulkier, with broader wings, a white supercilium, and a more rounded tail. Shikra (Accipiter badius) is casual (Cas) on the checklist; field identification is specialist. Kestrel and hobby are pointed-winged falcons, not hawks. This atlas does not yet have separate pages for those lookalikes.",
        "It is not a threat to people. Leave it alone. A small hawk appearing suddenly in a Georgian garden or park is more likely this species than a goshawk."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the Eurasian sparrowhawk found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2024 annotated checklist of the birds of Georgia lists it as a confirmed year-round resident and passage migrant (YR-R; M)."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In woodland, forest edge, parks, and towns, where it hunts small birds. It is named for Batsara-Babaneuri Protected Areas. In autumn the Batumi migration bottleneck is a main watchpoint, though birds pass singly. This page does not map every region."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "Short broad wings, long tail, yellow eye, flap-flap-glide. Male blue-grey above with rufous-barred breast; female larger and browner. Levant sparrowhawk has a dark eye and more pointed wings; goshawk is much bulkier."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "About 29–41 cm at species level. Females are clearly larger than males (about 185–350 g versus 105–196 g). A separate measured series from Georgia is not given on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Mainly small birds. Diet has not been studied separately in Georgia on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it present in Georgia?",
        "answer": "All year as a resident, plus spring and autumn passage, according to the 2024 checklist."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does it breed in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. YR-R on the 2024 checklist means resident breeder."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous or protected?",
        "answer": "It is harmless. It is globally Least Concern and coded LC nationally on the 2024 checklist. It is not on the 2014 Georgian Red List (Levant sparrowhawk is VU there). Do not catch wild birds."
      }
    ],
