    "facts": [
      "Described by Scopoli in 1769. The 2024 Georgian checklist follows IOC and the OSME Region List for names: order Strigiformes, family Strigidae, genus Athene. The English name on the 2024 checklist and SABUKO list is Little Owl.",
      "The subspecies expected in Georgia is A. n. indigena (Brehm, 1855). NPLG assigns this form to Georgia (“south-European little owl”), noting paler, greyer plumage than the nominate, with weaker belly contrast and weaker bars on the tail. Birds of the World places indigena from the Balkans and southern Ukraine through the Caucasus and Turkey. Field identification is to species.",
      "The usual Georgian name is ჭოტი (SABUKO, NPLG). ბუკნაჭო is also used in some Georgian sources. English names are little owl, and historically owl of Athena or owl of Minerva. In Georgian, ჭოტი is sometimes wrongly used as a synonym of eagle-owl (ზარნაშო); those are different species.",
      "2024 checklist codes: YR-R, data quality A, global and national Red List LC, occurrence quality H, evidence PH (photo). That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small compact owl with yellow eyes, a flat-topped head and no ear tufts. Confirmed in Georgia as a year-round resident on the 2024 checklist. Often seen by day; the usual confusion is Eurasian scops owl, which has ear tufts and is a breeding migrant.",
      "traits": [
        "Length at species level is often about 21–23 cm; wingspan in the literature is often cited around 50–56 cm; mass is often about 105–260 g. Published figures differ slightly. Females are typically heavier than males. The head is round and flat-topped, without ear tufts. The eyes are yellow. Pale “eyebrow” lines give a frowning look. The legs are relatively long for an owl; the tail is short.",
        "Upperparts are grey-brown to brown with whitish spots and bars. Underparts are paler with darker streaks or mottling. Juveniles are fluffier and less distinctly marked. Field identification is to species. By day it often perches openly on a post, roof, wire or ruin — unlike most forest owls. The repeated nasal song is unlike the single-note whistle of Eurasian scops owl.",
        "Eurasian scops owl (Otus scops) is similar in size but has ear tufts and is a breeding migrant on the 2024 checklist, not a year-round resident; this atlas has a separate page. Tengmalm's owl (Aegolius funereus) is a small forest owl with no ear tufts and yellow eyes, but a larger facial disc and denser woodland habitat; this atlas has a separate page. Tawny owl (Strix aluco) is much larger, with dark eyes and no ear tufts; this atlas has a separate page. Long-eared owl (Asio otus) is larger, with long ear tufts and orange eyes. Eurasian eagle-owl (Bubo bubo) is much larger; in Georgian the name ჭოტი is sometimes wrongly used for it. Barn owl (Tyto alba) has a heart-shaped face and is pale; it is on the 2014 Red List.",
        "It is not a threat to people. Do not handle it. A small yellow-eyed owl without ear tufts sitting openly by day in Georgian farmland or a village is this species, not eagle-owl."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is little owl found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2024 annotated checklist of the birds of Georgia treats it as a confirmed year-round resident and breeder (YR-R). Evidence includes photographs."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can it be found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In open and semi-open places: farmland with trees, orchards, rocky ground, steppes, ruins and village edges. This page has no municipality list and does not colour a regional map. Presence is confirmed at country level, not by region."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "Small size, yellow eyes, no ear tufts, spotted brown plumage. Often sits openly by day. Eurasian scops owl has ear tufts and does not stay through the Georgian winter."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "About 21–23 cm long at species level; wingspan in the literature is often about 50–56 cm. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Insects, earthworms and other invertebrates, plus small rodents, birds, lizards and frogs. Diet in Georgia is not separately studied on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Year-round, according to the 2024 checklist. It hunts mainly at dusk and at night, but is often visible by day on a perch."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does it breed in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. YR-R on the 2024 checklist means a breeding bird present throughout the year. The nest is a cavity in a tree, rock or building. Georgian clutch dates are not tabulated here."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous or protected?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. Do not approach. It is Least Concern globally and nationally on the 2024 checklist. It is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List. Do not catch wild birds."
      }
    ],
