    "facts": [
      "Linnaeus described it in 1758. The accepted name is Canis lupus. Order Carnivora, suborder Caniformia, family Canidae, genus Canis. The domestic dog (C. l. familiaris) is a domestic form of the same species; this page covers the wild wolf.",
      "Caucasus literature historically names C. l. cubanensis (Ognev, 1923). Pilot et al. 2014 do not treat Caucasian wolves as genetically isolated: haplotypes are shared with European and West Asian populations. Do not assign a Georgian individual to a subspecies in the field with confidence.",
      "In Georgian the usual names are მგელი and რუხი მგელი; some texts use ნაცრისფერი მგელი. In English: grey wolf or gray wolf; older texts also say timber wolf.",
      "IUCN lists Georgia as native. It is in Bukhnikashvili and Kandaurov’s 2002 annotated mammal list. Eliava et al. 2007 list it as LC. That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map.",
      "Kopaliani et al. 2014 document gene flow between wolves and livestock guarding dogs in Georgia: more than about 13% of sampled wolves had detectable dog ancestry. A hybrid may be less wary of people; that does not mean every wolf seen near a village is a hybrid."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A large dog-like predator with grey-brown, often mottled fur, erect triangular ears, a thick muzzle, and long legs. It is resident in Georgia.",
      "traits": [
        "At species level total length is often about 1.3–1.6 m, the tail about 30–50 cm, shoulder height often about 70–90 cm. Mass is wide: Mech 1974 gives about 18–55 kg for females and 20–80 kg for males; extreme northern males sit near the upper end. Caucasian animals are usually in the middle of that range. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page. Males are usually slightly larger than females. The muzzle is thick, the ears relatively short and erect.",
        "Colour is usually grey mixed with brown, black, and pale hair. The belly is paler. Fully black or nearly white morphs exist in the species, but a typical wild animal in Georgia is grey-brown. The tail hangs and is bushy; a white tip, as in the red fox, is not a reliable mark.",
        "The golden jackal (Canis aureus) also occurs in Georgia: much smaller, shorter-legged, more uniformly yellowish-grey, with a finer muzzle. The red fox (Vulpes vulpes) is smaller still, rusty-red, with black ear backs and often a white tail tip. A free-ranging dog can match a wolf in size and colour; a wolf typically has more erect ears, a thicker muzzle, longer legs, and larger feet, but a hybrid is not always identifiable in the field.",
        "It is not venomous. A bite or saliva can carry rabies risk. Do not handle it, especially if an animal approaches without fear or is aggressive. Daytime activity does not by itself mean rabies."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the grey wolf found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. IUCN lists it as native in Georgia. It is on national mammal checklists and on the list of hunting species."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "More in the mountains than in the lowlands. Named study areas include Kazbegi, Svaneti, Colchis, and the Lanchkhuti area. This page includes a field photograph from the David Gareja desert (7 December 2019). More sightings in the western lowlands, including near the Black Sea coast, have been noted in recent decades. This page has no municipality list. The atlas map is not coloured."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "A large, long-legged dog-like predator with grey-brown fur, a thick muzzle, and erect ears. A jackal is smaller and more yellowish; a fox is rusty-red and often has a white tail tip. Telling it from a dog is not always easy."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "At species level total length is often about 1.3–1.6 m; mass spans a wide range. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "It is a carnivore. In some parts of Georgia, including Kazbegi and Colchis, the literature names domestic ungulates as the main prey. Wild ungulates and hare also figure; a national menu has not been measured here."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "Mainly at dusk and at night. It can also appear by day. It is resident and does not migrate like many birds."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. It can bite. It kills livestock. In Georgia it is named among wild carnivores that can carry rabies. Reports of attacks on people exist, but frequency is not measured on this page. Do not handle it. After a bite, wash the wound and seek medical care."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "It is globally Least Concern and is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List. It is a hunting species; season and quota are set by official rules. International trade is regulated under CITES Appendix II."
      }
    ],
