    "facts": [
      "Linnaeus described it in 1758, originally as Cervus capreolus. The accepted name is Capreolus capreolus. Order Artiodactyla (even-toed ungulates), family Cervidae, subfamily Capreolinae, genus Capreolus.",
      "The genus has two living species: western C. capreolus and eastern C. pygargus. Historical subspecies include C. c. caucasicus (Dinnik, 1910). Lister and others treat caucasicus provisionally as the large form north of the Caucasus. Do not assign a Georgian individual to a subspecies in the field with confidence.",
      "In Georgian the usual names are შველი and ევროპული შველი. In English: European roe deer, western roe deer, roe deer. A fawn is a young of this species, not another deer.",
      "ASM lists Georgia as native. It is on national mammal checklists. That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small deer with a short tail and a white rump patch. Summer coat is rusty-brown, winter greyer. Only males grow antlers. It is resident in Georgia.",
      "traits": [
        "At species level (Sempéré et al. 1996, European samples) body length is about 107–126 cm, shoulder height 66–83 cm, mass 22–32 kg. The tail is short. The Agency’s Georgian page gives length about 150 cm and mass 10–25 kg; the English page gives 95–135 cm (excluding the tail) and 15–30 kg. The figures do not match. This page uses the Mammalian Species range for field size until a measured Georgian series is published. Males are usually slightly larger than females. Antlers are short, often with three tines on each side.",
        "Summer coat is rusty-brown; winter coat is greyer and thicker. The belly is paler. A white rump patch is a useful mark when the animal flees. Fawns have white spots on the back that fade after the first months. Females have no antlers.",
        "Red deer (Cervus elaphus) also occur in Georgia: much larger, longer- legged, with more complex antlers; they are listed as Endangered on the national Red List and remain mainly in protected areas. A roe deer is not a red-deer calf — they are separate species. Siberian roe deer (Capreolus pygargus) is larger and is not accepted here as part of Georgia’s fauna; the Agency names only C. capreolus. Bezoar goat (Capra aegagrus) has permanent curved horns, a beard in adult males, and a different outline on steep slopes. Wild boar (Sus scrofa) is heavier, with tusks not antlers, and a rounder print. Tur and chamois also differ in silhouette and habitat; this atlas does not yet have separate pages for red deer, tur, or chamois.",
        "It is not venomous. Do not handle it, especially a fawn. Watch from a distance."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the European roe deer found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. It is resident. The Agency of Wildlife names it as the only roe deer in Georgia. It is on national mammal checklists and on the hunting-species list."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "At woodland edges, in open deciduous or mixed forest, and in undergrowth. Camera traps confirm Mtirala, Kintrishi, and Machakhela, with the highest trapping rate in Mtirala. It is also named for Algeti, Borjomi-Kharagauli, Tusheti, and Kazbegi National Park. This page has no municipality list."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "A small deer with a short tail, rusty in summer and greyer in winter, and a white rump patch when it runs. Only males have short antlers. Red deer are much larger; bezoar goat has curved permanent horns."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "At species level shoulder height is about 66–83 cm, body length 107–126 cm, mass often 22–32 kg. Agency figures differ. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Plants — shoots, leaves, forbs, buds, and in winter more woody browse. Diet has not been studied separately in Georgia on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "Mainly at dusk and dawn. It can also appear by day. Adjara camera traps recorded more photos in spring and summer than in autumn and winter. It is resident and does not migrate like many birds. Fawns are born in late spring."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. It does not normally attack. Do not pick up a fawn. Control dogs. Drive carefully at night on forest roads."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "It is globally Least Concern. The Agency states it is not on Georgia’s Red List. It is listed as a hunting species; season and quota are set by official rules. Hunting in protected areas is prohibited unless a separate rule says otherwise. Poaching of forest ungulates remains a documented pressure in Georgia."
      }
    ],
