    "facts": [
      "Described in 1957 (Darevsky) as Lacerta saxicola dahli. Type locality: near Shagali, northern Armenia. The genus Darevskia follows Arribas 1997. Synonym: Lacerta dahli.",
      "The 2026 checklist voucher and COI barcode are from Kojori (CaBOL-ID 1027435). Mitochondrial DNA matches D. mixta and D. armeniaca because mixta is the maternal ancestor of both — a barcode is not a field colour mark.",
      "The name dahli honours the zoologist Sergei Konstantinovich Dahl. The Georgian name დალის ხვლიკი uses the same eponym."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small rock lizard. Colour is not enough. In Georgia, identify it by range, scalation, and an almost all-female population — not by dorsal colour.",
      "traits": [
        "The body is small and slender, the head flattened, the limbs well developed. In Georgian samples, adult snout–vent length is usually about 5.4–5.8 cm; literature maxima are about 63 mm. The tail is longer than the body and breaks easily. Do not handle a lizard to count scales.",
        "The back is grey, brown, or olive, often with darker spots or a network. The belly may be yellowish. Those tones occur in other rock lizards — colour does not identify the species. The 2026 figures from Kojori show head scalation and the preanal plates.",
        "A more reliable mark than colour is paired preanal plates. The Armenian rock lizard (D. armeniaca) has one very broad preanal plate, a larger body (max. about 74 mm), and a southern Georgian range (Ninotsminda / Saghamo, Akhalkalaki) — not Kojori. That contrast follows Darevsky 1967 and a 2017 comparison.",
        "The Kura lizard (D. portschinskii) often shares the same rocks. Size in Georgian samples is similar. The practical difference: portschinskii is sexual (males and females); dahli is almost only females. Reliable separation uses head scalation and the anal region; colour will not do it. D. mixta is a western Georgian endemic absent from Adjara — it almost never occurs with dahli."
      ]
    },
