    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small, ground-dwelling forest lizard with a head that is not flattened. Among Darevskia, colour is not enough — use habitat, head shape and range.",
      "traits": [
        "The head is not flattened like that of rock Darevskia. Dorsal scales are rounded, smooth or weakly keeled toward the rear. Femoral pores are few (often about 6–12) and do not reach the knee. Do not handle the lizard to count scales.",
        "The back is brown, olive or reddish-brown; pale flank stripes are common. Some animals have faint pale rings on the sides. Males often have a greenish belly with blue spots on the outer ventral plates; females are yellowish or whitish below. In juveniles the rear half of the tail is often turquoise-blue above. Colour does not separate species — it varies even within one population.",
        "Meadow lizards (Darevskia praticola and D. pontica) are also ground-dwellers but different lineages: praticola in eastern and central Georgia, pontica in the north-western Pontic area. Rock Darevskia (for example D. mixta, D. brauneri, D. adjarica) may share a valley but live on rock and have a flatter head. The sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) is larger and differently patterned. The Colchic slow worm (Anguis colchica) has no legs.",
        "The 2026 checklist’s Georgian name is კავკასიური ტყის ხვლიკი (Caucasian forest lizard); this atlas keeps Derjugin’s lizard / Artvin lizard — the type locality is Artvin in north-eastern Turkey. Described in 1898 (Nikolsky) as Lacerta derjugini. Genus Darevskia since 1997. D. mixta was once thought to be a hybrid of derjugini and a rock lizard; it is now a separate species."
      ]
    },
