    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small rock lizard of western humid gorges. Colour is not enough. Field ID needs scalation, size and range. It is absent from Adjara.",
      "traits": [
        "It lives on rock and stone, not as a ground forest lizard. Snout–vent length is often about 4.9–6.3 cm (Darevsky 1967; Gabelaia et al. 2015). Breeding males often have an emerald-green back and dark flanks. That is typical appearance and does not separate species by colour.",
        "A nearly diagnostic trait is one large scale (the post-central temporal, PCT) between the central temporal scale and the ear (tympanal) scale. Preanal plates are often paired; an unpaired preanal is more common in Greater Caucasus animals. Do not pick the lizard up to count scales. Rarely D. caucasica can show a similar PCT — scalation alone is not enough in a contact zone.",
        "Clark's rock lizard (D. clarkorum) is the closest relative but is geographically isolated — in the southwest, e.g. the Charnali Gorge; the Adjara candidate adjarica is in the parvula complex at Shuakhevi. mixta is absent from Adjara. It has a narrow contact zone with the Caucasian lizard (D. caucasica) in the Greater Caucasus; mtDNA is close — colour will not tell them apart.",
        "Derjugin’s lizard (D. derjugini) may occur in the same gorge but lives on the ground in forest, with a less flattened head — not on a rock face. mixta was once treated as a hybrid of derjugini and saxicola; it is now a separate species. Samegrelo also has other rock lizards (e.g. D. brauneri); colour will not separate those either. Checklist figures show head scalation and preanal plates (Sairme)."
      ]
    },
