    "overview": "The meadow lizard (Darevskia praticola) is a harmless, ground-dwelling lizard of eastern and central Georgia. It belongs to the true lizard family (Lacertidae). It has no venom and is not dangerous to people. The 2026 Georgian checklist treats it as biologically confirmed, not as a candidate species. Georgian populations here belong to the nominotypical lineage (D. p. praticola). Pontic populations of the northwest are treated separately in this atlas as the Pontic meadow lizard (Darevskia pontica), a candidate species. Snout–vent length at species level is usually under 6.5 cm. IUCN assessed it as Near Threatened in 2009. It is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List. Do not assume it throughout the country: humid Colchis and the Black Sea coast are pontica in this account, not this species.",
    "habitat": "It occurs in Georgia. Look for it at deciduous forest edges, clearings, grassy openings, and woodland meadows — on the ground and in leaf litter, not on rock faces. Most Darevskia live on rocks; this species and the Artvin lizard (D. derjugini) are exceptions. Tarkhnishvili et al. 2026 assign Georgian populations to eastern and central Georgia. Named recent points are Sioni Reservoir (head-scalation figure) and Sioni–Tushurebi (COI barcodes; Tushurebi is in Tianeti Municipality). Distribution confidence is medium. This atlas map marks Mtskheta — Mtianeti from those records; that does not mean the species is limited to two points. Older material indicates a wider eastern–central range: Lagodekhi (ZISP, 1980, Darevsky), the Tsiv-Gombori Ridge, the Surami Pass (ZISP, 1963, Shida Kartli), and the Kartli ridges (Muskhelishvili 1964–1970; Tuniyev et al. 2013). Those are historical and museum records; present status at every site is not re-checked on this page. Whether Armenian loriensis reaches adjacent eastern Georgia is, according to Tuniyev et al. 2013, still unclear — do not treat it as confirmed here. An exact Georgian elevational limit is not given in the checklist. In Armenia the nominotypical complex is reported from about 600–2,000 m in the forest belt; that is an Armenian interval, not a measured Georgian range. Do not search for this species on the Colchic lowland or the Black Sea coast.",
    "diet": "This page has no Georgian stomach-content study. In Bulgaria it eats mainly arthropods: often spiders, beetles, and leafhoppers; it usually avoids ants (Vacheva & Naumov 2022). That is a Balkan menu, not a list for Georgian sites. It seizes prey in the mouth; it has no venom.",
    "behavior": "It is active by day. It moves on the ground and basks at forest edges and in grass; in Serbian material activity may stop on very hot days as animals seek shade (Ćorović & Crnobrnja-Isailović 2018). That is a Balkan observation, not a Georgian hourly table. It lays eggs. In Serbia mating is in April–May, eggs are laid in June, one clutch a season, often 4–8 eggs (Ljubisavljević et al. 2008). In the Hyrcanian subspecies (Azerbaijan) clutches are 1–6 eggs. Neither calendar is measured for Georgian populations. If threatened it runs; if grabbed, the tail may break and the lizard may bite. Watch it and leave it where it is. Do not pick it up.",
