    "identification": {
      "summary": "A native water turtle with a low, often dark shell and yellow speckling. A high dome means tortoise; a red stripe behind the eye means the introduced slider.",
      "traits": [
        "The upper shell (carapace) is low and oval, not a high dome. Colour is usually dark olive, brown, or black, often with yellow dots or streaks. The lower shell (plastron) is paler. Emys has a movable hinge on the plastron; the Caspian turtle (Mauremys) has a practically rigid plastron.",
        "Head, neck, and legs are dark with yellow dots or short streaks. There is no broad red stripe behind the eye. The feet are webbed. For Kura-basin Caucasian forms Fritz (1998) gives a maximum shell length of about 18 cm, usually under 16 cm; a separate measured series for western Georgian nominate animals is not on this page. Males are often smaller than females, with a longer tail and a more concave plastron.",
        "The Mediterranean tortoise (Testudo graeca) is terrestrial: high-domed shell and columnar legs without webbing. The pond slider (Trachemys scripta, usually T. s. elegans) is introduced; it has a broad red stripe behind the eye and often bolder shell striping. The Caspian turtle (Mauremys caspica) is recorded from southeastern Georgia; it often has pale lengthwise neck stripes and a more striped than speckled shell.",
        "It is not venomous. Watch and do not pick it up. In city ponds, rule out the slider first, especially near Tbilisi and Batumi."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the European pond turtle found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2026 Georgian checklist treats it as a biologically confirmed native species."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can it be found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In freshwater ponds, lakes, and marshes. In the west, named points include Poti and Colchic coastal wetlands, including shore associated with Churia Fen. In the east the checklist assigns a subspecies, but this page does not shade eastern regions on the map."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "Low dark shell with yellow speckling, webbed feet, and no red ear stripe. A high dome is a tortoise; a red ear is a slider."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "In Caucasian Kura-basin forms the shell is often under 16 cm, maximum about 18 cm (Fritz 1998). A separate series for western Georgian populations is not given here."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I tell it from a red-eared slider?",
        "answer": "The slider is introduced; T. s. elegans has a broad red stripe behind the eye. The pond turtle is native and lacks that stripe."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I tell it from a Caspian turtle?",
        "answer": "The Caspian turtle is recorded from the southeast and often has pale lengthwise neck stripes. The pond turtle’s shell is usually darker with yellow speckling."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it venomous or dangerous?",
        "answer": "It has no venom. It may bite if handled. Do not pick it up. Wash a bite and seek care if needed."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "IUCN lists it as Near Threatened. It is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List."
      }
    ],
