    "facts": [
      "Described in 1832 (Ménétries); the type locality is usually given as Baku, not Georgia. Older literature often used Coluber ravergieri or Zamenis ravergieri. The modern genus Hemorrhois became standard after Nagy et al. 2004.",
      "The 2026 checklist specimen and COI barcodes are from Tmogvi (Aspindza).",
      "The Georgian name ნაირფერი მცურავი refers to the blotched dorsal pattern."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A slender, harmless racer with a round pupil and a blotched back. In Georgia the key points are dry rocky ground and the absence of a vertical pupil — this is not a viper and not a Levantine viper.",
      "traits": [
        "The body is slender and built for speed. The head is moderately distinct from the neck. The pupil is round. Dorsal scales are weakly keeled; there are often 21 rows at mid-body. Across the species, length may reach about 1.3 m. Do not pick the snake up to count scales.",
        "The ground colour is grey, brown, or sandy. The back has dark rhomboid or crossbar blotches and smaller spots on the sides. Toward the tail the blotches often join into lengthwise stripes. A dark diagonal streak sits below the eye; another runs from the eye toward the mouth corner. The belly is whitish, often with fine dark dots.",
        "Dahl’s whip snake (Platyceps najadum) is thinner, with neck ocelli rather than rhombs along the whole back. The Urartian ratsnake (Elaphe urartica) is stouter, with a dark head and larger rounded blotches. The steppe ratsnake (Elaphe dione) has a finer pattern and often lengthwise stripes. Adult red-bellied racers (Dolichophis schmidti) are often plain coppery with a red belly. The smooth snake is smaller and has smooth scales.",
        "A viper has a vertical pupil, a more triangular head, and strongly keeled scales; it is venomous. The Levantine viper (Macrovipera lebetina) is heavier, with a broad head and a vertical pupil — medically significant. If a blotched snake has a vertical pupil, it is a viper, not this species. There is no venom. A bite can still hurt. Do not pick it up."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Does the spotted whip snake occur in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2026 checklist treats it as biologically confirmed. The named locality is Aspindza, Tmogvi. It does not occur throughout the country."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where might I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "The named recent point is in Samtskhe — Javakheti, around Tmogvi. The 2021 survey links it to dry rocky or grassland habitats, mostly in the east, and notes a gain in knowledge. This atlas maps only Samtskhe — Javakheti. Named western or Colchic points are not listed here."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it venomous or dangerous to people?",
        "answer": "No. It has no venom. When threatened it usually flees. A large snake can bite — do not pick it up. A vertical pupil points to a viper or Levantine viper."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "Slender build, round pupil, grey or brown ground with rhomboid blotches that often become stripes on the tail, and a dark streak by the eye. Dahl’s whip snake has neck ocelli, not rhombs along the back. Vipers and the Levantine viper have a vertical pupil."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "Across the species, adults are about 1–1.3 m. A separate measured length for Georgian animals is not given on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat, and does it lay eggs?",
        "answer": "It lays eggs; outside Georgia the literature often cites 4–10 eggs. Diet has not been studied separately in Georgia; elsewhere it takes lizards, small mammals, birds, and other snakes."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "Across the range it is mainly diurnal. A separate hourly and seasonal calendar for Georgia has not been published; expect the warm season in dry rocky country."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does it differ from the Urartian ratsnake?",
        "answer": "The spotted whip snake is more slender and fast; the blotches are often rhomboid and turn into stripes on the tail. The Urartian ratsnake is stouter, with a dark head and larger rounded blotches. Both are harmless."
      }
    ],
