    "facts": [
      "Linnaeus described it in 1766. The accepted name is Mustela nivalis. Order Carnivora, suborder Caniformia, family Mustelidae, genus Mustela.",
      "Caucasus literature historically used two names: M. n. caucasica (Barrett-Hamilton, 1900) and M. n. dinniki (Satunin, 1907; type from Stavropol, held in the Museum of Georgia). Abramov and Baryshnikov 2000 treat dinniki as a synonym of caucasica and find little difference between the former “large” and “small” Caucasian forms. Georgian encyclopaedias still name two subspecies. Field identification is to species.",
      "In Georgian the usual names are დედოფალა and სინდიოფალა. In English: least weasel; in Europe often weasel or common weasel. In ethnographic dictionaries დედოფალა can also mean a doll; this page means only Mustela nivalis.",
      "IUCN lists Georgia as native. It is in Bukhnikashvili and Kandaurov’s 2002 annotated mammal list. Eliava et al. 2007 list it as LC. That is country-level confirmation plus the named points above, not a regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small, elongated brown predator with a white belly and a short tail without a black tip. It is resident in Georgia.",
      "traits": [
        "At species level head–body is about 13–26 cm, the tail about 1.5–9 cm, and mass often from a few tens of grams to about 250 g. Abramov and Baryshnikov give Caucasian (M. n. caucasica) males 176–265 mm head–body, with the tail about 30% of body length. Males are usually larger than females. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page. The head is flat and narrow, the neck long, the legs short, and the ears rounded.",
        "The back, flanks, outer legs, and tail are brown, from dark chocolate to pale sandy. The belly, throat, and inner legs are white. The brown–white line may be straight or irregular; Abramov notes mostly “nivalis”-type (straighter) colour in the Caucasus, with “vulgaris”-type also present. The tail tip may be slightly darker but is not black. That is the main field mark against the stoat (Mustela erminea).",
        "The stoat is on some Georgian checklists (Eliava et al. 2007, NE). It is larger and always has a black tail tip, including in the white winter coat. This atlas has no stoat page yet; frequency in Georgia is not mapped here. Beech marten (Martes foina) and pine marten (Martes martes) are much larger, with a bushy tail, and are more typically arboreal. A red fox is dog-like, not an elongated weasel.",
        "It is not venomous. Do not handle it. A small brown animal moving like a snake along a wall or in a garden, with a white belly, is more likely this species than a marten."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the least weasel found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. IUCN lists it as native in Georgia. It is on national mammal checklists."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In rank grass, scrub, farmland, woodland edges, and mountains where rodents are present. Named points are Borjomi-Kharagauli National Park (photographs, 25 May 2015), Lagodekhi, the Kvesheti–Kobi corridor, and one garden sighting in Telavi. This page does not map every region."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "Small elongated brown body, white belly, short tail without a black tip. A stoat has a black tail tip. A marten is much larger."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "At species level head–body is about 13–26 cm. Abramov gives Caucasian males 176–265 mm. There is no separate measured Georgian series on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Mainly small rodents. Diet has not been studied separately in Georgia on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it active?",
        "answer": "By day and at night. It is resident and does not migrate like many birds."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does it make a sound?",
        "answer": "Rarely heard. The clip on this page is from Spain (xeno-canto XC1146321) and is marked uncertain by the recordist; there is no Georgian recording here."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. It can bite if handled. Do not pick it up."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "It is globally Least Concern. Eliava et al. 2007 list it as LC in Georgia. It is not treated on this page as a threatened taxon on the 2014 Red List."
      }
    ],
