    "facts": [],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A medium brown frog with a horizontal pupil, dorsolateral folds and a dark mask behind the eye. It is the only Rana in Georgia. The usual confusion is the marsh frog.",
      "traits": [
        "The pupil is horizontal. A large dark temporal patch sits behind the eye — the usual mark of brown frogs. The back is olive, grey, brown, reddish or dark violet; the number of dark spots varies. A pale mid-dorsal stripe may be present or absent. The belly is usually unmarked, pinkish, orange-red or whitish. The hind legs are long (macrocnemis means a long shank); the heels overlap when the shins are set at right angles to the body. The inner metatarsal tubercle is oval and relatively small. AmphibiaWeb: males have internal vocal sacs and nuptial pads on the first finger. Adult length at species level is often about 4–8 cm: 40–79 mm in a Turkish series; breeding frogs in the Nedzura canyon exceed 50 mm before the second hibernation. This page has no separate Georgian size series.",
        "Two neighbouring forms are described in Georgia. Forest macrocnemis is smoother-skinned, with a pale spot pattern, often no mid-dorsal stripe, and a small metatarsal tubercle. Plateau camerani is more rugose, with a stronger dorsal spot pattern and a contrasting mid-dorsal stripe. Where the forms meet, for example on the northern Trialeti slopes, intermediate animals occur. That is a field difference, not evidence for two species in this atlas.",
        "The marsh frog (Pelophylax ridibundus) stays at water, is often green or olive and calls loudly; the brown frog is in forest or meadow away from the breeding season. The Caucasian parsley frog (Pelodytes caucasicus) is smaller, with a vertical pupil and an oblique cross on the back. The eastern spadefoot (Pelobates syriacus) is stocky, with a large digging spade, in the dry east. Toads are warty, with large glands behind the eyes. Tree frogs have toe pads and climb. The agile frog (Rana dalmatina) does not occur in Georgia — old records are treated as confusion with this species.",
        "It is not venomous. A brown frog with a horizontal pupil and a mask behind the eye at a forest-road puddle or a mountain pond matches this species more than a marsh frog. Do not handle it."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the Caucasian brown frog found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. Tarkhnishvili et al. 2026 list it as biologically confirmed. Barcodes are from Tabatskuri and Bjolieti (Guria)."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can it be found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In forest, subalpine meadow or beside mountain ponds, close to water. Named localities include the Colchis coast and western forests, Borjomi-Kharagauli and the Nedzura canyon, the southern Greater Caucasus (Kazbegi, Svaneti, Lagodekhi, Omalo) and the Javakheti plateau (Tabatskuri, Paravani, Akhalkalaki). Do not assume it on dry plains away from water."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "A brown or olive frog with a horizontal pupil, a dark mask behind the eye, dorsolateral folds and relatively long hind legs. It is not tied to open water like a marsh frog and does not have the parsley frog’s vertical pupil."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "Adults are often about 4–8 cm at species level. Breeding animals in the Nedzura canyon exceed 50 mm. This page has no separate Georgian size series."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is camerani a separate species?",
        "answer": "Not in this atlas. The checklist names two neighbouring subspecies; camerani rank may be revised, but the listed species remains R. macrocnemis."
      },
      {
        "question": "How does it differ from the marsh frog?",
        "answer": "This is Rana — a browner, more terrestrial frog with a mask behind the eye. The marsh frog (Pelophylax) stays at water and is often green."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous?",
        "answer": "It is not venomous. Do not handle it."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "Globally Least Concern (2023). CBG also lists LC on the Georgian Red List. Do not collect wild amphibians."
      }
    ],
