    "facts": [
      "Described by Linnaeus in 1758 as Columba turtur. It is now placed in Streptopelia (Bonaparte, 1855). The 2024 Georgian checklist follows the IOC World Bird List and the OSME Region List for names: order Columbiformes, family Columbidae, genus Streptopelia. English checklist name is European Turtle Dove.",
      "Four subspecies are widely recognised: S. t. turtur (Europe east through Asia Minor toward western Siberia), S. t. arenicola (North Africa and from Iraq–Iran east into Central Asia), plus more local Saharan and Nile forms (hoggara, rufescens). Handbook ranges place both turtur and arenicola in the broader Caucasus–Near East region. Field identification in Georgia is to species. A measured Georgian specimen series assigned to subspecies is not given on this page.",
      "Georgian is გვრიტი; ჩვეულებრივი გვრიტი appears in official conservation documents. English names in use are European turtle dove, European turtle-dove, and turtle dove. Russian is обыкновенная горлица.",
      "2024 checklist codes: BB; M, data quality A, global and national Red List VU, occurrence quality H, evidence PH; CH (photograph and checklist). That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map. Collared dove, oriental turtle dove, and laughing dove are listed as separate species on the same checklist."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A small migratory dove with chestnut-and-black scaly upperparts, a black-and-white striped neck patch, and a purring “turr-turr” song. In Georgia it is a breeding migrant on the 2024 checklist, Vulnerable globally and nationally on that list. The usual confusion is Eurasian collared dove.",
      "traits": [
        "Length about 26–28 cm and wingspan about 47–53 cm at species level. Mass is often given around 100–156 g. A separate measured series from Georgia is not given on this page. The body is slender for a dove. Upperparts are mottled chestnut and black (a scaly or tortoiseshell look). The head and underparts are blue-grey to pinkish-grey; the breast often shows a soft pink wash. Each side of the neck has a patch of fine black-and-white stripes. The eye has a reddish orbital ring in adults. The tail is dark with a clear white tip and sides, obvious in flight and when the bird fans the tail.",
        "Juveniles are duller and lack the neat neck patch until they moult. Sexes are similar in the field. The song is a soft, repeated purr — “turr-turr” — not the three-note coo of collared dove. Birds often feed on the ground in open patches and flush with a sharp wing rattle.",
        "Eurasian collared dove (Streptopelia decaocto) is a year-round resident and migrant in Georgia on the 2024 checklist (YR-R; M). It is paler, more uniform grey-buff, with a single black half-collar on the nape, not a striped side-neck patch, and it lacks the chestnut-black scaly back. Oriental turtle dove / rufous turtle dove (Streptopelia orientalis meena) is listed as a migrant only (M), with photographic evidence; it is larger and darker, with heavy scaling on the underparts and a different neck pattern — treat uncertain autumn birds carefully. Laughing dove (Spilopelia senegalensis) is coded as introduced and year-round resident (INT; YR-R): smaller, warmer pink-brown, with a spotted neck patch, not black-and-white stripes. Common woodpigeon (Columba palumbus) is much larger, with a white wing bar and neck patch of a different shape. This atlas does not yet have separate pages for those doves.",
        "It is not a threat to people. A small dove with a scaly chestnut back and a purring song in Georgia in late spring or summer is this species, not the pale collared dove of towns and villages."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is the European turtle dove found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. The 2024 annotated checklist of the birds of Georgia lists it as a confirmed breeding bird and migrant (BB; M). It is absent in the non-breeding season."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can I see it in Georgia?",
        "answer": "In the warmer months in open and semi-open country with scrub, hedges, woodland edge, or farmland with seed. This page has no named municipality list and does not colour the regional map. Presence is confirmed at country level, not mapped region by region here."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can I identify it?",
        "answer": "Chestnut-and-black scaly upperparts, black-and-white striped neck patch, white-edged dark tail, and a soft purring “turr-turr” song. Collared dove is paler with a single black nape collar and no scaly chestnut back."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large is it?",
        "answer": "About 26–28 cm long at species level, with a wingspan of about 47–53 cm. A separate measured series from Georgia is not given on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Mainly seeds taken on the ground; chicks are fed crop milk. Diet has not been studied separately in Georgia on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "When is it present in Georgia?",
        "answer": "As a breeding migrant: present in the breeding season and on passage, absent in winter, according to the 2024 checklist. Exact arrival and departure dates for Georgia are not tabulated on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "Does it breed in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. BB on the 2024 checklist means it is a confirmed breeder. Named nest sites inside Georgia are not listed on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it dangerous or protected?",
        "answer": "It is harmless to people. It is globally Vulnerable and coded VU nationally on the 2024 checklist. It is not on Georgia’s 2014 Red List (Decree No. 190). Do not catch or hunt wild turtle doves."
      }
    ],
