    "facts": [],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "Georgia’s only land tortoise. A high-domed shell, columnar legs, and thigh spurs separate it from pond turtles.",
      "traits": [
        "High-domed shell, not the flatter shell of a freshwater turtle. Colour ranges from yellowish-brown to dark brown, often with darker marks on the scutes. The exact pattern varies among individuals and is not enough to assign a subspecies in the field.",
        "Columnar legs with five claws in front and four behind. No webbed swimming feet. The head is rounded; the mouth is a horny beak, with no teeth.",
        "Usually one small conical keratinous spur on each thigh — the “spur-thighed” field mark. Georgia has no other land tortoise, so shell and legs are enough to separate it from water turtles.",
        "In eastern Georgia (Auer & Herz 2021) mean carapace length is 17.9 cm in males and 21.0 cm in females. Females are generally larger; males have a longer tail and a concave plastron. Juveniles are smaller, often with a stronger contrast in the pattern.",
        "The European pond turtle (Emys orbicularis), Caspian turtle (Mauremys caspica), and introduced slider (Trachemys scripta) are freshwater species: flatter shells, webbed feet, often basking on banks. T. s. elegans has a broad red stripe behind the eye. Testudo hermanni does not occur in Georgia.",
        "The checklist keeps two subspecies: T. g. ibera (east) and T. g. nikolskii (west, rare). Van der Kuyl et al. 2005 distinguish only graeca and ibera clearly; TFTSG 2023 treats nikolskii as a synonym of ibera. Tarkhnishvili et al. 2026 keep the name for phenotype and a distinct range; genetic distinction is not yet demonstrated. Do not assign a wild Georgian animal to a subspecies with certainty."
      ]
    },
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "Is it found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Yes. Tarkhnishvili et al. 2026 list it as a native, biologically confirmed species. It is Georgia’s only land tortoise."
      },
      {
        "question": "Where can it be found in Georgia?",
        "answer": "Mainly in dry, open country in the east. Named points include Gori, Tbilisi, Bolnisi, and Vashlovani. It is rare in the west. The map does not cover the whole country."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is this a water turtle?",
        "answer": "No. It is a land tortoise. Pond, Caspian, and slider turtles are freshwater species."
      },
      {
        "question": "How can it be identified?",
        "answer": "A high-domed shell, columnar unwebbed legs, and often a spur on each thigh. It walks slowly on land and does not bask on pond banks the way water turtles do."
      },
      {
        "question": "How large does it grow?",
        "answer": "In eastern Georgia carapace length is often about 15–25 cm; females are larger on average. Muskhelishvili 1970 cites a maximum of 26 cm."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it venomous or dangerous?",
        "answer": "It has no venom. It is harmless to people if left alone. It may bite if handled. Do not take it home."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does it eat?",
        "answer": "Mainly grasses and herbaceous plants. Diet has not been measured separately for Georgia on this page."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is it protected?",
        "answer": "Georgia’s 2014 Red List lists it as Vulnerable (VU). It is on CITES Appendix II. Collecting wild animals is prohibited or tightly restricted."
      }
    ],
