    "facts": [],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "Georgia’s largest newt. The skin is coarse. The belly is yellow or orange with large dark spots. Breeding males grow a high, jagged crest. Do not confuse it with the smooth newt or the banded newt.",
      "traits": [
        "The body is stocky, not slender. The skin is coarse and granular. In water the tail is flattened from side to side. Adults usually reach about 15–18 cm in total length; females are often slightly larger than males. Separate Georgian measurements are not given on this page.",
        "The back is brown to dull grey with darker patches. The belly and throat are yellow or orange with black spots; the spots may merge so that part of the belly looks black. There is no pale flank stripe. Females often have a narrow yellow line along the middle of the back.",
        "In the breeding season the male grows a high, jagged crest from the back onto the tail. The gap in the crest at the tail base is often weak or absent — unlike the northern crested newt (T. cristatus), which does not occur in Georgia. Females lack this crest.",
        "Lantz’s smooth newt (Lissotriton lantzi) is smaller (usually about 7–11 cm), with smoother skin and only a low male crest. The northern banded newt (Ommatotriton ophryticus) has a pale flank stripe and an unspotted belly. The Caucasian salamander (Mertensiella caucasica) has yellow spots, a round tail in cross-section, and lives by streams, not ponds.",
        "Older books listed Triturus cristatus karelinii. Today T. karelinii is a separate species of Crimea, the Caucasus and the southern Caspian. Balkan and western Anatolian crested newts are T. ivanbureschi and T. anatolicus and do not occur in Georgia. In the field they are almost impossible to tell apart by looks; in Georgia geography is enough."
      ]
    },
