    "facts": [
      "Described by Linnaeus in 1758. The 2024 Georgian checklist follows IOC / OSME Region List names: order Passeriformes, family Turdidae, genus Turdus. English names in use include common blackbird, Eurasian blackbird, and simply blackbird.",
      "Handbook treatments place the Caucasus, Transcaucasia, and northern Iran in subspecies T. m. aterrimus (type locality in the northern Caucasus). Birds of the World maps aterrimus through south-east Europe, Turkey, the Caucasus, and northern Iran. NPLG Wiki Dictionaries instead assign nominate T. m. merula to Georgia. Subspecies are hard to name in the field; treat birds in Georgia to species unless a specialist source assigns a form.",
      "The usual Georgian name is შავი შაშვი. English is common blackbird or Eurasian blackbird. Older or regional names include European blackbird.",
      "2024 checklist codes: YR-R, data quality A, global and national Red List LC, occurrence quality H, evidence PH (photograph), RD (ringing data), and SR (sound recording). That is country-level confirmation, not a regional map."
    ],
    "identification": {
      "summary": "A thrush-sized bird. Adult males are glossy black with a yellow-orange bill and a yellow eye-ring. Females are dark brown with a duller bill. In Georgia it is a year-round resident on the 2024 checklist. The usual confusion is other thrushes, especially ring ouzel in mountain habitat.",
      "traits": [
        "Length about 23.5–29 cm at species level; wingspan about 34–38.5 cm; mass commonly about 80–125 g. The bill is fairly long and straight. The tail is medium-long. Flight is often low and undulating with closed-wing dips.",
        "Adult males are black, with an orange-yellow bill and a yellow orbital ring. First-year males can look duller, with a darker bill and browner wings. Adult females are sooty- or chocolate-brown above, paler and mottled below, with a brownish bill. Juveniles are brown with pale spotting on the upperparts and a speckled breast.",
        "Ring ouzel (Turdus torquatus) is also a year-round resident in Georgia on the 2024 checklist; adult males show a clear white breast crescent and usually prefer higher, rockier or mountain habitats than garden blackbirds. Song thrush (Turdus philomelos) is smaller, brown above, with a boldly spotted pale breast — not solid black. Mistle thrush (Turdus viscivorus) is larger and greyer, with bold underpart spotting. Fieldfare (Turdus pilaris) is a migrant and winter visitor with a grey head and rump and chestnut back. This atlas does not yet have separate pages for those thrushes.",
        "It is not a threat to people. Leave it alone. A solid black garden thrush with a yellow-orange bill in Georgian towns and woodland edges is this species; a black mountain thrush with a white crescent is more likely ring ouzel."
      ]
    },
